source: src/bin/relish.rs
expression: help
---
=== relish run ===
Run a resource-aware task array from an image or allowlisted host binary.

Every task runs the same command with `{index}` in its arguments replaced by its own index, from 0 to COUNT-1, and gets RELIABURGER_TASK_INDEX, RELIABURGER_TASK_COUNT, RELIABURGER_TASK_ATTEMPT and RELIABURGER_BATCH_ID in its environment. With --image, the tasks run in containers; with --runtime process --exec, the binary must be in the nodes' [process_workloads] allowed_binaries.

Tasks run at least once, not exactly once: a task that finished just before its node crashed can run again. Make tasks safe to repeat.

Each submission prints an idempotency key before it is sent. If the submission times out, retry with --idempotency-key KEY: the cluster returns the run it already admitted rather than starting a second.

Usage: run [OPTIONS] --batch <NAME> [-- <ARGS>...]

Arguments:
  [ARGS]...
          Arguments after `--`; `{index}` becomes the task's index

Options:
      --batch <NAME>
          Name for the array (required: task arrays are the only kind of run so far)

      --count <COUNT>
          Number of tasks; one uses the same indexed executor
          
          [default: 1]

      --schedule <SCHEDULE>
          Register a durable UTC cron schedule (five fields, or a macro such as @daily) instead of running now

      --exec <EXEC>
          Host binary every task runs

      --image <IMAGE>
          OCI image executed by the owned Linux runtime

      --runtime <RUNTIME>
          Job backend: runc (default), process (requires --exec), or shared-runc

          Possible values:
          - runc:        A new owned container generation for each attempt
          - process:     An explicitly allowlisted host process, without a container image
          - shared-runc: A separate command process inside a compatible reusable container
          
          [default: runc]

      --cpu <CPU>
          CPU request-limit range, such as 250m-500m

      --memory <MEMORY>
          Memory request-limit range, such as 64Mi-128Mi

      --chunk <CHUNK>
          Tasks per chunk: the unit the cluster hands to nodes
          
          [default: 1024]

      --max-attempts <MAX_ATTEMPTS>
          Attempts per task before it counts as failed
          
          [default: 3]

      --max-failed <MAX_FAILED>
          Stop the whole array once more than this many tasks have failed

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Per-attempt timeout, in seconds; 0 means no deadline
          
          [default: 600]

      --concurrency <CONCURRENCY>
          Most tasks one node runs at once (default: 256, bounded by requests)

  -n, --namespace <NAMESPACE>
          Namespace the array belongs to
          
          [default: default]

      --env <KEY=VALUE>
          An environment variable for every task (repeatable)

      --idempotency-key <KEY>
          Reuse the key printed by an earlier submission that timed out

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

=== relish batch-status ===
Show the progress of a submitted batch

Usage: batch-status [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>
          Batch id from `relish batch`

Options:
      --wait
          Poll until the batch reaches a terminal state

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Give up waiting after this many seconds. This only bounds the wait: the run keeps going, under its own per-attempt timeout
          
          [default: 3660]

  -h, --help
          Print help

=== relish batch logs ===
Print a task's retained output (the first and last 2 KiB): any singleton's, or a failed task's in a larger array

Usage: logs --index <INDEX> <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>
          Batch id from `relish run --batch`

Options:
      --index <INDEX>
          The task's index

  -h, --help
          Print help

=== relish batch submit ===
Submit a compact TOML manifest containing mixed resource profiles

Usage: submit [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          

Options:
      --dry-run
          Validate the manifest locally without submitting work

      --idempotency-key <KEY>
          Reuse the key printed by an earlier submission that timed out, so the retry returns its runs instead of admitting the manifest again

  -h, --help
          Print help
